2026-06-30 15:48:29,492 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-06bff512-949c-45f4-9672-fcdbd0fdd735', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nE1047FD.raw\nE1048FD.raw\nE1049FD.raw\nE1050FD.raw\nE1051FD.raw\nE1052FD.raw\nE1053FD.raw\nE1054FD.raw\nE1055FD.raw\nE1056FD.raw\nE1057FD.raw\nE1058FD.raw\nE1059FD.raw\nE1060FD.raw\nE1061FD.raw\nE1062FD.raw\nE1063FD.raw\nE1064FD.raw\nE1065FD.raw\nE1066FD.raw\nE1067FD.raw\nE1068FD.raw\nE1069FD.raw\nE1070FD.raw\nE1071FD.raw\nE1072FD.raw\nE1073FD.raw\nE1074FD.raw\nE1075FD.raw\nE1076FD.raw\nE1985FD.raw\nE1986FD.raw\nE1987FD.raw\nE1988FD.raw\nE1989FD.raw\nE1990FD.raw\nE1991FD.raw\nE1992FD.raw\nE1993FD.raw\nE1994FD.raw\nE1995FD.raw\nE1996FD.raw\nE1997FD.raw\nE1998FD.raw\nE1999FD.raw\nE2000FD.raw\nE2001FD.raw\nE2002FD.raw\nE2003FD.raw\nE2004FD.raw\nE2005FD.raw\nE2006FD.raw\nE2007FD.raw\nE2008FD.raw\nE2009FD.raw\nE2010FD.raw\nE2011FD.raw\nE2012FD.raw\nE2013FD.raw\nE2014FD.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nAPEX2-based quantitative proteomic study of LAT and CD3z in Jurkat T cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTCR binding to its ligands induces a cascade of signaling starting by the phosphorylation of immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motif (ITAMs) present in the TCR-associated CD3 complex. This is followed by the phosphorylation of proteins including the adaptor LAT, which once phosphorylated interacts with multiple proteins allowing signal diversification and amplification downstream of the TCR. We take advantage of peroxidase-catalyzed proximity labeling combined with quantitative mass spectrometry to track the formation of CD3z and LAT interactome dynamics in Jurkat cells after TCR stimulation. We find more than 1 000 high confidence proteins for each bait and provide a quantitative molecular map of proteins enriched or reduced from the vicinity of CD3z and LAT, after stimulation. We detail and compare the recruitment kinetics of signaling proteins to CD3z and LAT and identify previously uncharacterized mediators of T cell activation. We show that the kinase MARK2, which is in the proximity of LAT and CD3z at resting state and lost upon activation, is a negative regulator of cytokine production by T cells. This study provides a resource for uncovering the complex signaling networks that regulate TCR activation and highlights new players of the TCR-induced signaling cascade.\n\n[METHODS]\nJurkat expressing the chimeric molecules: LAT-APEX or CD3z-APEX cells (20 × 106) were resuspended at 10× 106 cells/mL in RPMI medium + 10% FCS + 25mM HEPES + 0.5 mM Biotin-phenol (ref. LS-3500.0250, Iris Biotech) and incubated at 37°C + 5% CO2 for 1 hour. To activate the cells, we added anti-CD3 (clone OKT3, ref.317326, BioLegend) and anti-CD28 (clone CD28.2, ref.302934, BioLegend) antibodies at 4 μg/mL and incubated them at 37°C for different timepoints. One minute before the end of the activation, H2O2 was added at 0.1 mM, starting the biotin-tagging of proximal proteins. To stop the reaction, we added 2x freshly prepared cold quenching buffer 1:1 (1x = PBS with sodium azide 10 mM, sodium ascorbate 10mM [A4034-100G], Trolox 5mM [238813-5G]). Cells were then centrifuged at 400g for 3 min and washed with 4 mL of cold quenching buffer 1x 3 times. After the last centrifugation, cells are resuspended in 400 μL of Quenching lysis buffer (50mM Tris, 150 mM NaCl, 0.1% SDS, 0.5% sodium deoxycholate, 1% NP-40, 10mM sodium azide, 10 mM sodium ascorbate, 5mM trolox, protease inhibitor (cOmplete EDTA-free, ref 11873580001, Roche) and phosphatase inhibitor (Halt Phosphatase Inhibitor, ref. 78426, Thermo-Fisher Scientific)). After clearing the lysate by centrifuging at 20.000g at 4°C, we loaded the supernatant in a pre-washed Zeba Spin Desalting Column (7K MWCO, ref. 89890, Thermo-Fisher Scientific) and followed the manufacturer’s protocol to remove unreacted biotin-phenol. 10% of the lysates were kept as input and the biotinylated proteins present in the rest of the lysate were purified. 30 μL of pre-washed protease-resistant streptavidin Dynabeads M-280 were added to the samples and incubated at 4°C for 2.5 hours with gentle rotation. The beads were then washed sequentially: 2 times with quenching lysis buffer, 3 times with ammonium bicarbonate 200mM (Sigma, ref. A6141) + 6M Urea (Euromedex, ref. EU0014.A) and 5 times with ammonium bicarbonate 25 mM. Half of the beads were resuspended in 100 μL of ammonium bicarbonate 25 mM and delivered to the proteomic platform, and the other half was resuspended in 20 μL of laemmli buffer (Bio-Rad, ref. 1610747) + 2mM biotin (Sigma, ref. B4501) + 20 mM DTT (Pierce, ref. A39255) for WB analysis.  After delivery to the proteomic platform, the beads were washed trice with 100 μL of 100 mM NH4HCO3, resuspended in 100 μL of 100 mM NH4HCO3 and digested by adding 0.2\u2009μg of trypsin-LysC (Promega) for 1\u2009h at 37 °C. Samples were then loaded into custom-made C18 StageTips packed by stacking three AttractSPE® disk (#SPE-Disks-Bio-C18-100.47.20 Affinisep) into a 200 µL micropipette tip for desalting. Peptides were eluted using a ratio of 40:60 CH3CN:H2O\u2009+\u20090.1% formic acid and vacuum concentrated to dryness with a SpeedVac device. Peptides were reconstituted in 10µL of injection buffer in 0.3% TFA before LC-MS/MS analysis.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
